# enemies design rows: three duel tiers as designer data (Bycer ch8).
# Designers change numbers here without a Rust edit; the AI seat loads
# these rows into tier skill (GA13) and the game test below checks them.
# Whole numbers only, `#` comments, no floats. Units live in the key:
# `_permille` is parts per thousand, `_ticks` is fixed ticks at 60 Hz.
# Columns: hp_permille (narrow 900-1200 band, skill first), tell_ticks
# (readable startup, 15 or more), stagger_permille (poise hold 0-1000,
# higher holds longer), counter (1 parry, 2 dodge, 3 block: the answer
# from the player's own kit that beats this tier).
# Cards live in docs/design/ENEMIES.md: tier_1 is the Holder grunt
# (teaches parry), tier_2 is the Flanker veteran (teaches dodge),
# tier_3 is the Holder elite (stamina pressure, riposte). One melee
# enemy at a time per encounter; StrikePhase is the shared 15/1/8 clock.
# House numbers: the 60% tier bar (a tier beats the one below in 60% or
# more of seeded duels) and the 2-4 tick human noise stay until the
# tournament and human tapes prove another number.
# T4 boss stays PROPOSED (needs owner EB-2026-10-12-S32); no tier_4 row
# lands before that answer.
table enemies version 1
tier_1 hp_permille=950 tell_ticks=15 stagger_permille=400 counter=1
tier_2 hp_permille=1000 tell_ticks=15 stagger_permille=500 counter=2
tier_3 hp_permille=1050 tell_ticks=15 stagger_permille=600 counter=1
